namespace Ponca
{
/*!
  \page fitting Fitting module: user manual

  \tableofcontents

  \section fitting_intro Introduction

  The Fitting module estimates local geometric properties of point clouds. Given an evaluation position
  \f$\mathbf{p}\f$ and a scale \f$t\f$, it fits a simple *primitive* (plane, line, sphere, quadratic height
  field, ...) to the neighbors of \f$\mathbf{p}\f$, each neighbor being weighted according to its distance to
  \f$\mathbf{p}\f$. The fitted primitive then answers geometric questions: normal vector, distance to the underlying
  surface, projection onto it, curvatures, and how these quantities vary when \f$\mathbf{p}\f$ or \f$t\f$ change.

  \ref figure1 "Figure 1(a)" shows a typical example in 2D: a potential function (in false colors) is reconstructed
  from a few 2D points equipped with normals; the 0-isoline of this potential is an implicit curve (in orange) from
  which further properties, such as the curvature, are extracted. The approach works in arbitrary dimension
  \cite Guennebaud:2007:APSS. Figures 1(b-c) show an implicit 3D surface reconstructed in the same way from a 3D point
  cloud. Meshes can be processed by considering their vertices only.

  \anchor figure1
  \image html interpolation.png "Figure 1: (a) An implicit 2D curve fitted to a 2D point cloud. (b) A 3D point cloud shown with splats. (c) An implicit 3D surface reconstructed from (b)."

  The module provides:
  - the fitting of planes, lines, algebraic spheres (from points or oriented points \cite Guennebaud:2007:APSS, or
    from non-oriented points \cite Chen:2013:NOMG) and quadratic height fields (Monge patches),
  - descriptors computed from these primitives: surface variation and covariance features
    \cite Pauly:2002:PSSimplification \cite Guinard:2017, Growing Least Squares parameters \cite Mellado:2012:GLS,
    curvature tensors \cite Lejemble:2021:stable, Corrected Normal Current curvatures \cite pointCloudCNC-sgp23,
  - the derivatives of these quantities with respect to the scale and to the evaluation position,
  - a Moving Least Squares (MLS) projection operator \cite Alexa:2004:projection,
  - all of this in arbitrary dimension for most methods, on the CPU or on GPUs (CUDA, SYCL).

  This page explains how to use the module. The complete list of methods and of their outputs is given in the
  \ref fittingreference, curvature estimation is detailed in \ref fitting_curvature_page, and the definition of new
  computations in \ref fitting_extending_page. Before reading further, make sure you know how to describe your data,
  see \ref ponca_points.

  \section fitting_firstSteps First steps

  \subsection fitting_codeStructure Include directive

  The Fitting module is header-only, works on the CPU and in CUDA kernels, and depends only on Eigen. Include its
  header:
  \code
  #include <Ponca/Fitting>
  \endcode
  or `<Ponca/Ponca>` to include all the modules.

  \subsection fitting_Define Definition of the fitting object

  A fitting object is a specialization of the class template Ponca::Basket:
  \code
  Basket<Point, NeighborFilter, FittingProcedure, Extensions...>
  \endcode

  1. `Point` is the point type (see \ref ponca_points), for instance:
     \snippet basket.cpp SpecializedPointType
  2. `NeighborFilter` defines how the neighbors are weighted. Most of the time, it is a Ponca::DistWeightFilter: a
     neighbor \f$\mathbf{q}\f$ is given the weight \f$\phi(\|\mathbf{q}-\mathbf{p}\| / t)\f$, where \f$\phi\f$ is a
     weight kernel, here \f$\phi(x) = (x^2-1)^2\f$ (Ponca::SmoothWeightKernel), see
     \ref figure2 "Figure 2(a)":
     \snippet basket.cpp NeighborFilter
  3. `FittingProcedure` defines the primitive and how it is fitted. Here, Ponca::OrientedSphereFit
     \cite Guennebaud:2007:APSS fits an algebraic sphere (Ponca::AlgebraicSphere) to points equipped with normals:
     \snippet basket.cpp FitType
  4. Optional extensions add computations performed on top of the primitive. For instance, Ponca::GLSParam
     \cite Mellado:2012:GLS computes the Growing Least Squares parameters of the fitted sphere:
     \code
     using Fit = Basket<Point, NeighborFilter, OrientedSphereFit, GLSParam>;
     \endcode

  The available filters are described in \ref fitting_neighborhoods, and the fitting procedures in
  \ref fitting_primitives.

  \subsection fitting_Fitting Fitting process

  The neighbor filter is set first, from the evaluation position \f$\mathbf{p}\f$ and the scale \f$t\f$. The
  neighbors are then given to the fitting object, which computes the primitive:
  \snippet basket.cpp Fit Compute

  `compute(container)` (or `compute(begin, end)` with iterators) visits all the points of the container: points
  farther than \f$t\f$ get a null weight and are ignored. On large point clouds, it is much more efficient to collect
  the neighbors first with a spatial data structure (see \ref spatialpartitioning), and to give their indices to
  `computeWithIds`:
  \snippet basket.cpp Fit computeWithIds

  \note The query and the neighbor filter are independent objects: make sure that they use the same position and
  radius. Points returned by the query but farther than \f$t\f$ are ignored by the filter; points that are not returned
  by the query are never considered.

  Internally, `compute` and `computeWithIds` call `init()`, then `addNeighbor()` for each point, then `finalize()`,
  and repeat these steps when the method needs several passes over the neighborhood (see \ref fitting_custom).

  \subsection fitting_Checkstatus Check fitting status

  `compute`, `computeWithIds` and `finalize` return a Ponca::FIT_RESULT, which can also be queried later with
  `getCurrentState()`, `isReady()` and `isStable()`. Always check it before using the results:

  | Value                       | `isReady()` | Meaning                                                    | Typical cause |
  |-----------------------------|-------------|------------------------------------------------------------|---------------|
  | Ponca::STABLE               | yes         | the fit is ready to use                                    |               |
  | Ponca::UNSTABLE             | yes         | the fit is ready to use, but considered unreliable         | sphere fits with fewer than \f$2\times\f$`Dim` neighbors |
  | Ponca::UNDEFINED            | no          | the fit failed, its results are meaningless (possibly NaN) | no neighbor with a positive weight, fewer than `Dim` neighbors for covariance and sphere fits, solver failure, degenerate configuration |
  | Ponca::NEED_OTHER_PASS      | no          | another pass over the neighbors is required                | multi-pass methods (Monge patches) when neighbors are added manually |
  | Ponca::CONFLICT_ERROR_FOUND | no          | ill-formed basket                                          | two procedures computed the same primitive, see \ref fitting_multiprimitive |

  \code
  Ponca::FIT_RESULT res = fit.compute(points);
  if (res == Ponca::STABLE) // equivalent to fit.isStable()
  {
      // use the results
  }
  std::cout << fit.getNumNeighbors() << " neighbors, sum of the weights: " << fit.getWeightSum() << std::endl;
  \endcode

  \subsection fitting_outputs Basic outputs

  \anchor figure2
  \image html gls.png "Figure 2. (a) Fitting a 2D point cloud of positions and normals at a point p (in red) requires to define a weight function of size t (in green). (b) This results in an implicit scalar field (in false colors), from which parameters of a local spherical surface can be extracted: an offset tau, a normal eta and a curvature kappa."

  A fitted basket gives access to the functions of all the classes it aggregates. For the basket defined above:
  \code
  using Point      = Ponca::PointPositionNormal<double, 3>;
  using VectorType = Point::VectorType;
  using Filter     = Ponca::DistWeightFilter<Point, Ponca::SmoothWeightKernel<double>>;
  using Fit        = Ponca::Basket<Point, Filter, Ponca::OrientedSphereFit, Ponca::GLSParam>;

  Fit fit;
  fit.setNeighborFilter({p, t});
  if (fit.compute(points) == Ponca::STABLE)
  {
      // Implicit primitive: the scalar field and its zero isosurface
      double     f = fit.potential(q);         // value of the scalar field at q
      VectorType g = fit.primitiveGradient(q); // gradient of the scalar field at q (not normalized)
      VectorType x = fit.project(q);           // projection of q onto the fitted surface

      // Ponca::AlgebraicSphere
      VectorType c = fit.center();
      double     r = fit.radius();

      // Ponca::GLSParam: offset, normal and curvature at the evaluation position
      double     tau   = fit.tau();
      VectorType eta   = fit.eta();
      double     kappa = fit.kappa();
  }
  \endcode

  - All the positions given to and returned by these functions are expressed in the coordinate system of the point
    cloud (see \ref fitting_frames).
  - Implicit primitives define a scalar field whose zero isosurface is the fitted surface. Its sign may be meaningless:
    `fit.isSigned()` tells whether the method can orient the field (e.g. covariance plane fits cannot, see
    \ref fittingreference_primitiveoverview).
  - \ref fittingreference_outputandcapabilities lists the functions available for each method.

  \section fitting_neighborhoods Neighborhoods and weights

  \subsection fitting_neighborhoods_filters Neighbor filters

  The neighbor filter is constructed for each evaluation from a position \f$\mathbf{p}\f$ and a scale \f$t\f$
  (`fit.setNeighborFilter({p, t})`). For every neighbor, it computes a weight and the coordinates of the neighbor in a
  local frame:

  | Filter                                       | Weight of a neighbor \f$\mathbf{q}\f$                                  | Local coordinates |
  |----------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------|
  | Ponca::DistWeightFilter<Point, Kernel>       | \f$\phi(\|\mathbf{q}-\mathbf{p}\|/t)\f$, 0 beyond \f$t\f$ for compact kernels | \f$\mathbf{q}-\mathbf{p}\f$ |
  | Ponca::NoWeightFilter<Point>                 | 1 for every point                                                      | \f$\mathbf{q}-\mathbf{p}\f$ |
  | Ponca::NoWeightFilterGlobal<Point>           | 1 for every point                                                      | \f$\mathbf{q}\f$  |

  Ponca::NoWeightFilter and Ponca::NoWeightFilterGlobal do not limit the neighborhood: they are meant to process a set
  of neighbors selected beforehand (e.g. by a k-nearest neighbors query). Ponca::FilterWithAttributes extends a filter
  with additional data, for instance the normal at the evaluation position used by Ponca::CNC. All the filters are
  described in \ref fitting_filters.

  \subsection fitting_neighborhoods_kernels Weight kernels

  The kernel \f$\phi\f$ of Ponca::DistWeightFilter controls how the influence of a neighbor decreases with its
  distance:
  - Ponca::SmoothWeightKernel, \f$\phi(x) = (x^2-1)^2\f$, is the default choice: smooth, compact, and fast to evaluate,
  - Ponca::ConstantWeightKernel gives the same weight to all the points of the ball of radius \f$t\f$,
  - Ponca::WendlandWeightKernel and Ponca::CompactExpWeightKernel are smoother compact kernels,
  - Ponca::GaussianWeightKernel is not compact: every point contributes, which requires to bound the neighborhood
    with a spatial query. As its standard deviation is \f$t\f$, it is wider than a compact kernel of radius \f$t\f$,
  - Ponca::SingularWeightKernel tends to infinity when the distance tends to 0, which makes the fitted surface
    interpolate the points \cite Alexa:2009:Hermite,
  - Ponca::PolynomialSmoothWeightKernel generalizes the smooth kernel.

  Their expressions are given in \ref fitting_kernel. The kernels used to compute derivatives must provide valid
  derivatives (see \ref concepts_weighting).

  \subsection fitting_neighborhoods_scale Choosing the scale

  The scale \f$t\f$ is expressed in the units of the point cloud. For compact kernels, it is the radius of the
  neighborhood. It drives a trade-off: small scales are sensitive to noise and to the sampling density (with too few
  neighbors, fits become Ponca::UNSTABLE or Ponca::UNDEFINED), while large scales smooth out the details of the
  surface and bias the estimates. A few tens of neighbors with a positive weight is a reasonable starting point.

  Rather than choosing a single scale, the scale can also be considered as a parameter of the analysis: Growing Least
  Squares \cite Mellado:2012:GLS study the evolution of the fitted sphere when \f$t\f$ grows, using the scale
  derivatives described in \ref fitting_derivatives.

  \subsection fitting_frames Local frames and coordinates

  Filters derived from Ponca::CenteredNeighborhoodFrame (Ponca::DistWeightFilter, Ponca::NoWeightFilter) express the
  neighbors relatively to the evaluation position, and the primitives are stored in this local frame. This keeps the
  values close to 0 and improves the accuracy of the computations. The conversions are handled internally: the
  functions of the fitted object take and return positions in the global frame.

  The fitted primitive is thus attached to the evaluation position: `getNeighborFrame().center()` returns it, and the
  implicit primitives can be re-expressed relatively to another position with `changeBasis(newCenter)` (this is what
  the MLS projection does at each iteration).

  \warning Even with centered frames, avoid data with very high or very low magnitudes (e.g. georeferenced coordinates,
  or a point cloud whose extent is \f$10^{-6}\f$): translate and rescale the data globally beforehand.

  \section fitting_primitives Primitives and fitting procedures

  A basket aggregates several *computational objects*. Fitting a plane with Ponca::CovariancePlaneFit, for
  instance, involves:
  - a primitive, Ponca::Plane, which stores the result and provides the potential, the gradient and the projection,
  - tools accumulating statistics over the neighborhood: Ponca::MeanPosition (barycenter) and Ponca::CovarianceBase
    (covariance matrix),
  - a fitting procedure, Ponca::CovariancePlaneFitImpl, which computes the plane from these statistics.

  The aliases used in the examples combine these objects. For instance:
  \snippet covariancePlaneFit.h CovariancePlaneFit Definition
  \snippet orientedSphereFit.h OrientedSphereFit Definition

  The main fitting procedures are:

  | Procedure                                   | Primitive              | Input                         | Dimension | Notes |
  |---------------------------------------------|------------------------|-------------------------------|-----------|-------|
  | Ponca::CovariancePlaneFit                   | Ponca::Plane           | positions                     | any       | plane of least squares orthogonal distance (PCA); the orientation of the normal is arbitrary |
  | Ponca::MeanPlaneFit                         | Ponca::Plane           | positions and normals         | any       | plane through the barycenter, orthogonal to the mean normal |
  | Ponca::CovarianceLineFit                    | Ponca::Line            | positions                     | any       | line of largest variance; the potential is the squared distance to the line |
  | Ponca::SphereFit                            | Ponca::AlgebraicSphere | positions                     | any       | algebraic sphere fit \cite Guennebaud:2007:APSS, host only |
  | Ponca::OrientedSphereFit                    | Ponca::AlgebraicSphere | positions and normals         | any       | APSS \cite Guennebaud:2007:APSS, closed form, oriented |
  | Ponca::UnorientedSphereFit                  | Ponca::AlgebraicSphere | positions, unoriented normals | any       | \cite Chen:2013:NOMG, generalized eigenvalue problem, not available with CUDA |
  | Ponca::MongePatchQuadraticFit               | Ponca::MongePatch      | positions                     | 3D        | quadratic height field over the PCA plane (two passes), provides curvatures |
  | Ponca::MongePatchRestrictedQuadraticFit     | Ponca::MongePatch      | positions                     | 3D        | same, without linear terms |
  | Ponca::CNC<Point, Method>                   | none                   | positions and normals         | 3D        | Corrected Normal Current curvatures \cite pointCloudCNC-sgp23; not a basket, see \ref fitting_curvature_page |

  Ponca::MeanPosition, Ponca::MeanNormal and Ponca::DryFit (which only counts the neighbors) can also be used alone.
  The requirements and outputs of every class are listed in \ref fittingreference.

  Each computational object provides a *cast function* returning the basket seen as this object, e.g.
  `fit.plane()`, `fit.algebraicSphere()`, `fit.meanPosition()`, `fit.covarianceBase()` or `fit.glsParam()`. Cast
  functions are useful when a function name is shared by several classes of the basket, and they make the code more
  explicit:
  \code
  const auto& sphere = fit.algebraicSphere(); // the Ponca::AlgebraicSphere part of the basket
  double uq          = sphere.uq();           // quadratic coefficient of the sphere
  VectorType b       = fit.meanPosition().barycenter();
  \endcode
  Generic cast functions are also defined for the capabilities (`implicitPrimitive()`, `projectionOperator()`,
  `curvatureTensor()`, ...), see \ref fittingreference_outputandcapabilities.

  \section evaluation_schemes Evaluation schemes and MLS projection

  A single fit approximates the surface around one evaluation position. *Moving Least Squares* (MLS) surfaces
  \cite Levin:1998 \cite Cheng:2008 are defined implicitly by a projection operator: a point is projected onto the
  primitive fitted around it, the fit is computed again at the projected position, and so on until convergence
  \cite Alexa:2004:projection. Ponca::MLSEvaluationScheme implements these iterations for any fitting object
  providing an implicit primitive:
  \snippet mls.cpp MLS Fit

  For instance, to project a position `q` onto the MLS surface defined by an oriented sphere fit:
  \code
  using Fit = Ponca::Basket<Point, Filter, Ponca::OrientedSphereFit>;

  Fit fit;
  fit.setNeighborFilter({q, t});                    // starting position and scale
  Ponca::MLSEvaluationScheme<double> mls(10, 1e-8); // at most 10 iterations, tolerance on the displacement
  if (mls.compute(fit, points) == Ponca::STABLE)
  {
      VectorType x = fit.getNeighborFrame().center(); // MLS projection of q
      VectorType n = fit.primitiveGradient(x).normalized();
  }
  \endcode

  - `MLSEvaluationScheme<Scalar>(nIter, eps)`: at most `nIter` iterations (5 by default); the iterations stop when the
    projected position moves by less than `eps` (relative, `Eigen::NumTraits<Scalar>::dummy_precision()` by default),
    or when a fit is not stable.
  - At each iteration, the evaluation position of the fit is moved to the last projected position: at the end,
    `fit.getNeighborFrame().center()` holds the projected position and the fit holds the primitive fitted there.
  - The projection operator is a parameter of `compute`: Ponca::DirectProjectionOperator (default) uses the projection
    of the primitive, Ponca::GradientDescentProjectionOperator follows the gradient of the scalar field and works with
    any implicit primitive:
    \code
    mls.compute(fit, points, Ponca::GradientDescentProjectionOperator{});
    \endcode
  - `computeWithIds(fit, ids, points)` uses the same neighbors at every iteration: they are not queried again when the
    evaluation position moves. Query them with a radius larger than \f$t\f$ if the position is expected to move.

  Ponca::SingleEvaluationScheme has the same interface but performs a single fit, which makes it possible to write
  code that is generic with respect to the evaluation scheme:
  \snippet evaluationScheme.h SingleEvaluationScheme Compute Definition

  \section fitting_derivatives Computing derivatives

  A fit computed at the position \f$\mathbf{p}\f$ and scale \f$t\f$ is a function of \f$(\mathbf{p}, t)\f$ (the
  neighbors being fixed). Ponca::BasketDiff extends a basket to compute the derivatives of the fitted quantities with
  respect to the scale (Ponca::FitScaleDer), to the evaluation position (Ponca::FitSpaceDer), or both
  (Ponca::FitScaleSpaceDer). Its template parameters are the basket to differentiate, the differentiation type, and
  the extensions computing the derivatives:
  \snippet basket.cpp PlaneFitDerTypes

  Each fitting procedure has its own derivative extension, and the extensions are listed from the most basic to the
  most derived (an extension can only use the extensions listed before it):

  | Extension                                 | Differentiates                                     | Requires |
  |-------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------|----------|
  | Ponca::CovariancePlaneDer                 | Ponca::CovariancePlaneFit (3D only)                | |
  | Ponca::OrientedSphereDer                  | Ponca::OrientedSphereFit                           | |
  | Ponca::UnorientedSphereDer                | Ponca::UnorientedSphereFit                         | |
  | Ponca::MlsSphereFitDer                    | second order derivatives of the algebraic sphere   | Ponca::OrientedSphereDer or Ponca::UnorientedSphereDer, points with normals |
  | Ponca::GLSDer                             | GLS parameters, geometric variation \cite Mellado:2012:GLS | a sphere derivative and Ponca::GLSParam |
  | Ponca::NormalDerivativeWeingartenEstimator, Ponca::WeingartenCurvatureEstimatorDer | curvature tensor from the normal derivatives | a derivative providing `dNormal()`, space derivatives, 3D |

  \subsection fitting_derivatives_layout Layout of the derivatives

  The derivatives are stored in fixed-size arrays with one entry (or one column) per variable:
  `ScalarArray` is a row vector for scalar quantities, and `VectorArray` is a `Dim` x N matrix for vector quantities.

  | Differentiation type      | Number of derivatives N | Indices                                                      |
  |---------------------------|-------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------|
  | Ponca::FitScaleDer        | 1                       | [0]: derivative with respect to the scale                    |
  | Ponca::FitSpaceDer        | `Dim`                   | [0, `Dim`): derivatives with respect to the evaluation position |
  | Ponca::FitScaleSpaceDer   | 1 + `Dim`               | [0]: scale, [1, `Dim` + 1): evaluation position              |

  \code
  using PlaneFit  = Ponca::Basket<Point, Filter, Ponca::CovariancePlaneFit>;
  using PlaneDiff = Ponca::BasketDiff<PlaneFit, Ponca::FitScaleSpaceDer, Ponca::CovariancePlaneDer>;

  PlaneDiff fit;
  fit.setNeighborFilter({p, t});
  fit.compute(points);
  PlaneDiff::ScalarArray dPotential = fit.dPotential(); // 1 x 4: d/dt, d/dx, d/dy, d/dz
  PlaneDiff::VectorArray dNormal    = fit.dNormal();    // 3 x 4: one column per variable
  double     dPotential_dt = dPotential(0);
  VectorType dNormal_dt    = dNormal.col(0);
  \endcode

  The GLS parameters are differentiated by Ponca::GLSDer (`dtau()`, `deta()`, `dkappa()` and their scale-normalized
  versions), which also computes the *geometric variation* \cite Mellado:2012:GLS from the scale derivatives:
  \code
  using GLSFit  = Ponca::Basket<Point, Filter, Ponca::OrientedSphereFit, Ponca::GLSParam>;
  using GLSDiff = Ponca::BasketDiff<GLSFit, Ponca::FitScaleDer, Ponca::OrientedSphereDer, Ponca::GLSDer>;

  GLSDiff fit;
  fit.setNeighborFilter({p, t});
  fit.compute(points);
  double dkappa_dt = fit.dkappa()(0);
  double nu        = fit.geomVar(1, 1, 1); // weights of the tau, eta and kappa terms
  \endcode
  \warning Give the weights of `geomVar` explicitly: without argument, `fit.geomVar()` refers to the cast function of
  Ponca::GLSDer, not to the geometric variation.

  \section fitting_curvature_sec Curvature estimation

  Mean curvatures, principal curvatures and principal directions can be estimated with several methods, presented
  in a dedicated page: \subpage fitting_curvature_page.

  \section fitting_advanced Advanced usage

  \subsection fitting_factory Iterating over common baskets: the Factory

  Comparing several methods usually requires to write a function template and to call it for every basket type.
  Ponca::Factory lists the most common compute objects for a given point type, neighbor filter and derivative type,
  and iterates over them:
  \snippet ponca_factory.cpp FactoryBasicForeach

  The list can be filtered by capabilities: for the main capability concepts `ProvidesXXX` (see
  \ref fittingreference_outputandcapabilities), the factory defines the predicates `XXXProvider` and
  `NotXXXProvider` (listed in \ref fittingreference_factory). Several predicates are combined with a logical *and*.
  Filtered lists store their objects, so their state persists between two calls to `foreach` when the objects are
  taken by reference:
  \snippet ponca_factory.cpp FactoryFilterPersistent

  Compute objects can also be retrieved by name:
  \snippet ponca_factory.cpp FactoryClassicalMethods

  The names are listed in \ref fittingreference_factory. The content of the factory depends on its parameters: the
  Monge patches and CNC estimators are only listed for 3D points, and `ASO` requires space derivatives.

  \subsection fitting_multiprimitive Fitting with multiple primitives

  A basket usually contains one primitive, and the helper aliases (e.g. Ponca::CovariancePlaneFit,
  Ponca::OrientedSphereFit) should be preferred. It is however possible to fit several primitives in the same basket,
  for instance to share intermediate computations. The arrangement of the computational objects must then be given
  explicitly:
  \snippet basket.cpp HybridType

  After fitting, both primitives are accessed through their cast functions, `fit.algebraicSphere()` and
  `fit.plane()`.

  The capability concepts check that the requirements of every class are met, but they do not detect two procedures
  writing the same primitive, e.g.:
  \snippet fit_plane.cpp Conflicting type
  This case is detected when finalizing the fit, which returns Ponca::CONFLICT_ERROR_FOUND: each procedure checks
  that its primitive has not already been computed.

  \subsection fitting_custom Changing the fitting process

  `compute` is equivalent to the following loop:
  \snippet basket.cpp Fit Manual Traversal

  Methods that need several passes over the neighborhood (e.g. Ponca::MongePatchQuadraticFit, which first fits a
  plane, then a height field over it) return Ponca::NEED_OTHER_PASS from `finalize()`. When neighbors are added
  manually, repeat the traversal, calling `startNewPass()` before each pass:
  \code
  fit.setNeighborFilter({p, t});
  fit.init();
  Ponca::FIT_RESULT res;
  do
  {
      fit.startNewPass();
      for (const auto& q : points)
          fit.addNeighbor(q);
      res = fit.finalize();
  } while (res == Ponca::NEED_OTHER_PASS);
  \endcode

  Writing the traversal explicitly makes it possible to use any data structure, or any rule to select the neighbors.

  \subsection fitting_extending_sec Extending the module

  New weight kernels, neighbor filters and computational objects can be defined and combined with the existing ones:
  see \subpage fitting_extending_page.

  \section fitting_cuda CUDA and SYCL

  The fitting objects can be used in CUDA and SYCL kernels, with the same code as on the CPU: functions are marked
  with `PONCA_MULTIARCH`, and Eigen is used in its device-compatible subset. A few methods rely on host-only code
  (Ponca::SphereFit, the Monge patches and Ponca::CNC), and Ponca::UnorientedSphereFit is not available when
  compiling with nvcc. Compilation options, data transfers and examples are given in \ref ponca_gpu_page.
 */
} // namespace Ponca
